{"name":"Crystal Ball","hex":"#365955","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crystal ball","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A globe of glass or crystal that is used to foretell the future.","A manner or method of seeing into the future."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crystal_ball"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystal ball","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal ball is a crystal or glass ball commonly used in fortune-telling. It is generally associated with the performance of clairvoyance and scrying through crystal gazing. Used since Antiquity, crystal balls have had a broad reputation with witchcraft, including modern times with charlatan acts and amusements at circus venues, festivals, etc.\nOther names for the object include crystal sphere, orbuculum, scrying ball, shew/show(ing) stone, and more variants by dialect."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/A_crystal_ball_at_work.jpg/330px-A_crystal_ball_at_work.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_ball","wikidata":"Q1032349"},{"title":"Crystal Ball (box set)","blocks":[{"text":"Crystal Ball is a box set by American recording artist Prince. The set includes the eponymous Crystal Ball, Prince's twentieth studio album and second triple album, which consists of \"previously bootlegged\" material, as well as The Truth, Prince's 21st studio album."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/87/Crystall_Ball_%28Prince_box_set_-_cover_art%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_Ball_(box_set)","wikidata":"Q3005991"},{"title":"Crystal","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal or crystalline solid is a solid material whose constituents are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their geometrical shape, consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as crystallography. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called crystallization or solidification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Quartz_28.jpg/330px-Quartz_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal","wikidata":"Q43533"},{"title":"Ball","blocks":[{"text":"A ball is a round object. Balls can have several uses. For example they are used in ball games, where the play of the game follows the state of the ball as it is hit, kicked or thrown by players. Balls can also be used for simpler activities, such as catch or juggling. Balls made from hard-wearing materials are used in engineering applications to provide very low friction bearings, known as ball bearings. Black-powder weapons use stone and metal balls as projectiles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Many_balls.jpg/330px-Many_balls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ball","wikidata":"Q18545"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Crystal_Ball.JPG/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Crystal_Ball.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Crystal_Ball.JPG/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Crystal_Ball.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Crystal Ball by John William Waterhouse (1902)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Crystal_Ball.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb